  "isLocked": true,
  "description": "Unpivot a DataFrame from wide format to long tidy format with identifier variables and variable-value columns.\n\nWide tables with measurements in multiple columns violate first normal form. Melting into long format aligns datasets with tidy data standards for regression modeling.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\n\\text{pd.melt}(\\text{df}, \\text{id\\_vars}, \\text{value\\_vars})\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Preserve identifying columns listed in id_vars\n2. Unpivot columns listed in value_vars into rows\n3. Name melted columns var_name and value_name and return list of dictionaries\n\n### Returns\nList of unpivoted record dictionaries.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: Melt Wide to Long\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nReal-world tabular datasets are frequently messy, containing missing values, duplicates, and unaligned columns. Pandas provides powerful DataFrame primitives for reshaping, filtering, and cleaning structured data before training machine learning models. **Melt Wide to Long** performs structured data manipulation efficiently.\n\nWide tables with measurements in multiple columns violate first normal form. Melting into long format aligns datasets with tidy data standards for regression modeling. In this problem, the goal is to: unpivot a dataframe from wide format to long tidy format with identifier variables and variable-value columns.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\n\\text{pd.melt}(\\text{df}, \\text{id\\_vars}, \\text{value\\_vars})\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Preserve identifying columns listed in id_vars\n2. **Phase 2**: Unpivot columns listed in value_vars into rows\n3. **Phase 3**: Name melted columns var_name and value_name and return list of dictionaries\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Vectorized Array Operations**: Applying vectorized transformations operates on contiguous C memory buffers under the hood, running orders of magnitude faster than iterating with Python loops.\n* **Data Cleansing**: Handling duplicates and missing data prevents bias and data leakage into downstream machine learning models.\n* **Memory Management**: Choosing appropriate column dtypes (e.g. categorical or int32 instead of object) drastically reduces DataFrame memory usage.",
  "examples": [
    "Input: records = [{\"dept\": \"Eng\", \"Q1\": 100, \"Q2\": 150}], id_vars = [\"dept\"], value_vars = [\"Q1\", \"Q2\"], var_name = \"quarter\", value_name = \"revenue\"\n\nOutput: [{\"dept\": \"Eng\", \"quarter\": \"Q1\", \"revenue\": 100}, {\"dept\": \"Eng\", \"quarter\": \"Q2\", \"revenue\": 150}]\n\nExplanation: Evaluated pandas_melt_wide_to_long under standard test conditions."
